---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE, url } from '../config/site';
const home = new URL(url(), Astro.site).href;
const jsonld = { '@context': 'https://schema.org', '@type': 'AboutPage', name: `About ${SITE.name}`, url: new URL(url('about/'), Astro.site).href, inLanguage: 'en-AU',
  description: 'Gov Score holds the Albanese Government to account on its economic record, using official figures. What it is for, and the standards it keeps.',
  mainEntity: { '@type': 'Organization', name: SITE.name, url: home, description: 'The Albanese Government’s economic record in official figures.' } };
---
<Base title="About Gov Score" jsonld={jsonld} description="Gov Score holds the Albanese Government to account on its economic record, using official figures. What it is for, and the standards it keeps." ogImage={url('share/page/about.png')} ogImageAlt="About Gov Score">
  <div class="wrap-narrow pt-10 md:pt-14">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1 class="display mt-2 text-[clamp(34px,4.6vw,52px)]">Why this site exists</h1>
    <div class="prose mt-6">
      <p>Australians are paying more for groceries, power, rent and mortgages, while the federal government spends more, borrows more and keeps missing its own targets. {SITE.name} puts that record in one place, in plain English, so anyone can see it for themselves.</p>
      <p>We are upfront about what this is. {SITE.name} is a record of where the Albanese Government has fallen short. It is not a neutral scorecard, and it doesn’t pretend to be. What makes it worth trusting is the evidence: the numbers are the government’s own.</p>
      <h2>Our standards</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Official figures, every time.</strong> Every number links to the ABS, Reserve Bank, Treasury, Budget paper or agency release it came from. The one exception is market rents for new leases, which no agency publishes; that figure comes from Cotality and is labelled as such.</li>
        <li><strong>Their words, their deadlines.</strong> Promises are judged against what the government itself said it would do, by when.</li>
        <li><strong>Quotes are attributed.</strong> Commentary from journalists, economists and MPs is quoted with the name, the outlet and a link, never paraphrased into something they didn’t say.</li>
        <li><strong>Plain words.</strong> No jargon without an explanation.</li>
        <li><strong>Mistakes are fixed in public.</strong> If a figure doesn’t match its source, it is corrected and logged on the <a href={url('corrections/')}>corrections page</a>.</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>What’s on the site, and what isn’t</h2>
      <p>The site concentrates on the economy and the Budget: cost of living, housing, jobs, productivity, spending, debt and net worth, plus the promises the government made about them, its backflips and its controversies. Measures that have moved the government’s way, and social-policy measures, are not carried here.</p>
      <h2>How the site works, in one table</h2>
      <table>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Data sources</th><td>ABS, Reserve Bank, Treasury and Budget papers, AOFM, program agencies; IMF and World Bank for G20 comparisons; Cotality for market rents. Listed on every page.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Automated checks</th><td>A daily program fetches the official series, recalculates every figure and runs hundreds of tests. A failed test holds the last verified figure back. <a href={url('methodology/#checks')}>What “checked” means</a>.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Human review</th><td>Promises, hand-entered figures, backflips and controversies are checked by a person against the source, and dated.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Verdict rules</th><td>Published in full on the <a href={url('methodology/')}>methodology page</a>.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Data downloads</th><td>Every series as CSV and JSON on the <a href={url('data/')}>data page</a>.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <h2>Using the figures</h2>
      <p>Every measure page has a “Copy citation” button and a list of sources. If you are quoting a figure in a speech, a post or on a doorstep, quote the number with its source and date, exactly as shown. That way nobody can knock it down.</p>
      <h2>Get in touch</h2>
      <p>Use the <a href={url('corrections/#report')}>report form</a> for errors or anything else. Every message is read.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</Base>
